import "lib/list.spp" as L
//%% [markdown]
// # What are you looking at?
//
// <p class="beat">A presentation?</p>
//%% [markdown]
// # What are you looking at?
//
// <p class="beat dim">A presentation?</p>
// <p class="beat">Or a program?</p>
//%%
sieve (p : rest) = p : sieve [ x | x <- rest, x % p != 0 ]

primes =: sieve (L.from 2)
//%%
n = 12
//%%
L.take n primes
//%% [markdown]
// # Both.
//
// An **interactive document** that runs **entirely in your browser**:
// no server, no installation, and you can **edit it on the fly**.
//%%
D.slider 10 200 n
//%%
primesUpTo x = L.length (L.takeWhile (\p -> p <= x) primes)
//%%
D.lineChart [ primesUpTo x | x <- L.range 2 n ]
//%% [markdown]
// <p class="caption">Number of primes up to <i>n</i></p>
//%% [markdown]
// ## What just happened?
//
// - Every cell is a small program, **compiled when you change it** and linked
//   into the running session; only the cells that depend on it run again.
// - The compiler, the linker and the typechecker are **themselves bytecode**,
//   running on **one small VM** in WebAssembly, in this browser tab.
// - The infinite list `primes` is computed **lazily**: only as far as a cell asks.
//
// <p class="hint">Press <b>C</b> to show the code, change it, and watch the slide follow.</p>
//%%
D.table ["Step, per cell", "Runs where", "Written in"]
  [ ["Sapl+ preprocessor (records, ZF, patterns)", "this tab", "Sapl"],
    ["Typechecker", "this tab", "Sapl"],
    ["Compiler gen1 (to bytecode)", "this tab", "Sapl"],
    ["Linker, loading into the session", "this tab", "Sapl"],
    ["The VM itself", "this tab (WebAssembly)", "C"] ]
//%% [markdown]
// ## Where it started: IFL 2016
//
// <div class="twocol">
// <div>
// <p><em>A Portable VM-based Implementation Platform for Non-strict Functional Programming Languages</em></p>
// <ul>
// <li>A small VM for lazy functional languages: the <b>JMVM</b>, about <b>1,500 lines of C</b></li>
// <li>A small core language, <b>Sapl</b>, with a compiler of about <b>1,000 lines of Amanda</b></li>
// <li>Runs in the <b>browser</b>, and stays within a <b>factor of 5</b> of Clean and Haskell</li>
// </ul>
// <p class="hint">A prototype. The question in July 2026: how far can one person take it, with LLMs?</p>
// </div>
// <img src="afscheid/paper_first_page.png" alt="First page of the IFL 2016 paper">
// </div>
//%% [markdown]
// ## Why was this a good starting point?
//
// Two choices in the 2016 prototype turned out to matter far more than we knew at the time:
//
// 1. **A structured intermediate form** between the language and the VM: what we now call **Retag**.
// 2. **Laziness isolated in two instructions**, `eval` and `jmpt`. The rest of the VM is a plain,
//    eager stack machine.
//
// <p class="hint">We only found out later how much of the follow-up work rests on these two choices.</p>
//%% [markdown]
// ## 1. Retag: laziness made explicit
//
// <div class="twocol even">
// <div>
//
// **Sapl** (the `primes` benchmark)
//
// ```
// filter p xs = case xs (Nil -> Nil)
//   (Cons a as -> if (p a)
//      (Cons a (filter p as))
//      (filter p as))
//
// from a = Cons a (from (a+1))
//
// sieve xs = case xs nomatch
//   (Cons a as ->
//      Cons a (sieve (filter (nmz a) as)))
//
// primes = sieve (from 2)
// ```
//
// </div>
// <div>
//
// **Retag** (after strictness analysis and lifting)
//
// ```
// filter p !xs = case xs (Nil -> Nil)
//   (Cons a as -> if (eval(p a))
//      (Cons a (thunk (filter p as)))
//      (filter p (eval(as))))
//
// from a = Cons (eval(a))
//   (thunk (from (thunk (from_1 a))))
//
// sieve !xs = case xs (Nil -> nomatch)
//   (Cons a as -> Cons a (thunk
//      (sieve (thunk (filter (closure (nmz a)) as)))))
//
// primes = sieve (from 2)
// ```
//
// </div>
// </div>
//
// Every decision about laziness is **visible in the code**: `eval` (evaluate now), `thunk` (later),
// `closure` (partial application), `!` (strict argument). A backend only has to follow it.
//%% [markdown]
// ## 2. Laziness isolated: `eval` and `jmpt`
//
// <div class="twocol even">
// <div>
//
// - **`eval`**: the *only* place where a thunk is reduced. It loops on the VM's own control
//   stack, so deep laziness never touches the C stack.
// - **`jmpt`**: a jump table on the constructor of an evaluated value: `case` in one instruction.
// - Everything else (arithmetic, comparisons, calls, building nodes) is **eager** and knows
//   nothing about laziness.
//
// <p class="hint">The JMVM code of <code>sieve</code>, just to show it:</p>
//
// </div>
// <div>
//
// ```
// 22  load 0               || sieve_lazy
//     eval
//     store 0
// 23  load 0               || sieve
//     jmpt 2 24 25
// 24  push "sieve_Nil"     || sieve_Nil
//     debug 2
//     return 1
// 25  load 1               || sieve_Cons
//     load 0
//     pushfunc 2 12        || nmz_lazy
//     create 2 7
//     pushfunc 2 15        || filter_lazy
//     create 3 3
//     pushfunc 1 22        || sieve_lazy
//     create 2 3
//     load 0
//     ccreatet 2 11 5 1
//     return 3
// ```
//
// </div>
// </div>
//%% [markdown]
// ## The key turned out to be Retag, not the VM
//
// Almost everything built since starts from **Retag**, not from the bytecode:
//
// - **Native code**: Retag to C (`sapl2c`), on par with GHC `-O2`.
// - **Clean on the JMVM**: ABC decompiled back to Retag (`abc2retag`).
// - **Haskell lite**, Sapl+ and Core Sapl: all lower to the same Retag.
// - **Linking modules** and the **optimiser** work on Retag.
//
// <p class="beat">In Clean, ABC code is the hub.<br>Here it is one level higher.</p>
//
// <p class="hint">Retag was never designed as an interface: in 2016 it only existed to explain the compiler in the paper.</p>
//
// <p class="hint">Same clang, two inputs: C from bytecode was 2.2× slower than Clean native; C from Retag is faster (0.84×).</p>
//%% [markdown]
// ## Four months, step by step
//%%
D.table ["When (2026)", "Step"]
  [ ["17 July", "Start from the 2016 prototype: VM in C, compiler in Amanda"],
    ["12 August", "The compiler, now written in Sapl, compiles itself: self-hosting"],
    ["25-26 September", "It becomes the production compiler; the first notebook"],
    ["7-11 October", "REPL and tools in Sapl, not in the VM; binary .jvb format"],
    ["14 October", "A typechecker on every compile"],
    ["23 October", "Notebooks complete: forms, interactive cells, values kept"],
    ["1-8 November", "Typed Haskell anchor, superinstructions, Haskell lite, Clean via ABC"],
    ["11-13 November", "Native code: Retag to C"] ]
//%% [markdown]
// ## Self-hosting: the compiler compiles itself
//
// - From **19 July** the compiler was rewritten **in Sapl itself** (gen1), next to the Amanda original.
// - **12 August**: the fixpoint. gen1 compiles its own source to exactly the same bytecode.
// - **25-26 September**: gen1 becomes the production compiler; the code it generates is as fast as or
//   faster than the old compiler's on every benchmark.
// - **1 November**: a **typed Haskell version**, generated from gen1, takes over as the independent
//   anchor. The Amanda compiler is frozen.
//
// <p class="hint">From then on, all language work happens in Sapl, on the VM, and can be checked byte for byte.</p>
//%% [markdown]
// ## The turning point: "Can we do something useful with it?"
//
// Two days at the end of September:
//
// - **25 September**: an overview of all REPLs ends with a chapter *Applications*: teaching lazy
//   evaluation, a Jupyter-like kernel, playgrounds in the browser, exploring data.
// - **The same evening**: a plan for *notebooks, libraries and qualified imports*.
// - **26 September**: the first Sapl notebook, together with libraries for statistics, plots and CSV.
//
// <p class="beat">You are looking at the result.</p>
//%% [markdown]
// ## The consequence: tools in Sapl, not in the VM
//
// - **7-8 October**: the REPL becomes **one Sapl program** instead of three hosts in C++, Python and
//   JavaScript. The VM only offers a few primitives.
// - **9-11 October**: a **binary load format** (`.jvb`): the VM no longer reads text at all.
// - The compiler, linker, typechecker and REPL all run as **bytecode**: unchanged in the browser,
//   natively, and on a server.
//%%
D.table ["Effect of .jvb", "Before", "After"]
  [ ["Start the REPL", "~117 ms", "~6 ms"],
    ["Compile a small program", "~40 ms", "~12 ms"] ]
//%% [markdown]
// <p class="hint">A rule that stuck: extend the VM only for a real, frequent need without alternative;
// everything else becomes a Sapl module.</p>
//%% [markdown]
// ## Then: more languages and backends, all through Retag
//
// - <span class="dropped">**MicroHs**, a real Haskell compiler, bootstrapped on the JMVM: hard going, not continued.</span>
// - **Haskell lite** in its place: type classes, records and monads, translated to Sapl+, running in the browser.
// - **Clean**: ABC code from the Clean compiler, decompiled back to Retag (`abc2retag`).
// - **Native code**: Retag to C (`sapl2c`), on par with GHC `-O2`.
//
// <p class="hint">Each new frontend or backend only had to meet Retag, not every other part.</p>
//%% [markdown]
// ## How fast is the interpreter?
//%%
D.table ["The JMVM interpreter (Apple M4, 9 Nov)", "Result"]
  [ ["vs. Clean native, 12 benchmarks (total time)", "2.5× slower"],
    ["vs. GHC -O2, 12 benchmarks (total time)", "3.1× slower"],
    ["vs. Clean's ABC interpreter, native", "1.25× slower"],
    ["In the browser (WebAssembly, Chrome) vs. native", "1.47× slower"],
    ["In the browser vs. Clean's ABC interpreter (stock wasm, Node)", "1.43× faster"],
    ["The compiler compiling itself: interpreter vs. GHC -O2", "598 vs. 295 ms (2.0×)"] ]
//%% [markdown]
// - The 2016 target was **within a factor of 5** of Clean and Haskell. Natively it is now **2.5-3×**.
// - In the browser it loses **less than 1.5×** against itself natively, and it is faster than the
//   ABC interpreter that Clean ships for the browser today.
//
// <p class="hint">Sources: performance factsheet; experiments/clean_vergelijking/meting_2026-11-09. Clean native in a Linux VM.</p>
//%% [markdown]
// ## How could one person build this?
//
// About **four months** of work, since 17 July 2026:
//%%
D.table ["Since 17 July 2026", "Count"]
  [ ["Commits (plus 215 merges)", "1,213"],
    ["Lines of Sapl / Sapl+ (compiler, tools, libraries)", "~138,000"],
    ["Lines of VM (C)", "~21,000"],
    ["Design documents and reports", "153"] ]
//%% [markdown]
// - **One small VM** in C; everything else is a program running on it.
// - A **self-hosting compiler** (gen1) that compiles itself, anchored by a typed Haskell version.
// - One intermediate form, **Retag**, shared by every route: Sapl, Haskell lite and Clean.
// - **LLMs** made experiments cheap: five backends were built and *measured*, instead of argued about.
//%% [markdown]
// ## Dead ends, and why that was fine
//%%
D.table ["Experiment", "What happened", "Replaced by"]
  [ ["Amanda-to-Sapl translator (August)", "Partly worked; a hang in the full bridge was never found", "The compiler written from scratch in Sapl"],
    ["A JIT to WebAssembly (September)", "Correct, but slower than the warm interpreter", "The plain interpreter in wasm"],
    ["Bytecode to C, jmvm2c (November)", "2.2× slower than Clean native, 47 s builds", "Retag to C, sapl2c"],
    ["Whole-program Retag optimiser", "Doubled the compiler, four subtle bugs", "One local rule in gen1"],
    ["MicroHs on the JMVM (November)", "Bootstrapped, but heavy and server-bound", "Haskell lite, in the browser"] ]
//%% [markdown]
// - Starting again **from scratch** usually went better than repairing the first attempt.
// - With LLMs, **throwing work away is cheap**: you did not spend weeks typing it yourself.
//%% [markdown]
// ## The real insight: verification, not speed
//
// LLM-written code became trustworthy through **oracles that check byte for byte**:
//
// - the **fixpoint** of gen1: the compiler must reproduce itself exactly;
// - the **typed Haskell version** as an independent anchor;
// - **two generators** (Python and Sapl) that must emit the same C;
// - the **full regression** on every change, and the documents first.
//
// <p class="hint">What went wrong, honestly: an agent reported 38 ms for the compiler compiling itself.
// It was an aborted run; the fixpoint check caught it.</p>
//%% [markdown]
// ## What this means for Clean
//
// 1. **Lead with the toolchain.** One small VM with everything language-related as
//    bytecode, on both sides of the wire. That answers "Clean in the browser".
// 2. **Native code is an add-on.** Via Retag to C, without touching the VM:
//    faster than Clean native on small programs, ~1.3× slower on large typed code.
// 3. **The level of the intermediate form matters more than the backend.** Bring
//    stack code (ABC, JMVM bytecode) back to a structured form, and keep the types.
//%% [markdown]
// ## Measured
//%%
D.table ["Measurement (Apple M4)", "Result", "Date"]
  [ ["Sapl via Retag to C vs. Clean native, 12 benchmarks", "0.84×", "14 Nov"],
    ["Sapl via Retag to C vs. GHC -O2, 13 benchmarks", "0.99×", "14 Nov"],
    ["Clean via ABC, Retag and C vs. Clean native, 12 benchmarks", "0.95×", "13 Nov"],
    ["Typed Clean compiler (2,700 functions) compiling itself", "223 vs. 163 ms (1.37×)", "13-14 Nov"],
    ["  the same, without tag checks", "211 ms (1.30×)", "14 Nov"],
    ["GHC on the same task, default vs. tuned nursery", "271 vs. 226 ms", "13 Nov"],
    ["Clean's ABC interpreter, C via Emscripten vs. generated wasm", "0.71× (preliminary, V8)", "14 Nov"] ]
//%% [markdown]
// <p class="hint">Lower is faster. Clean native ran in a Linux VM on the same machine.</p>
//%% [markdown]
// ## What has not been measured
//
// - A real **iTasks** application: speed, and **compile time** (the typed self-compile
//   is 3.7 MB of C and takes 4.2 s in clang).
// - **Uniqueness and arrays**, records, `ccall`, dynamics.
// - The native route in **WebAssembly**, and graph compatibility with the ABC interpreter.
// - **Firefox and Safari** for the ABC interpreter variants; Windows.
//%% [markdown]
// ## A proposal
//
// One day together: take **one real iTasks program** through the abc2retag route,
// and measure speed, compile time and graph compatibility.
